{"text": "\u2018TIMN 0131486\n\nEl\n\n\u2018JOURNAL -HEUS |\nWest NVACK, KY\n\noatLy 42,268\n\nonoay\n\nDEC 31 1990\n\n\u2018New Yons Stare Cuppmc Senvice\nS10 Fy\n\nOUR VIEW: \u00ae\n\nClearing the air\n\nTt\n\nTobacco industry can help prevent teen smoking\n\nOULD it be that the\ntobacco industry has\nfinally decided to\n\nstop blowing smoke\nabout its concern for young\n\u2018smokers? Not necessarily.\nStill, it\u2019s certainly good to see\nthe industry starting to do\nsomething about deterring\n\u2018them, whatever the motive.\n\n\u2018Tobacco product\nmanufacturers recently\nlaunched an estimated $10\nmillion campaign they say is\naimed at curbing smoking\namong minors-These youths -\nare the same people the\nindustry, which is seeing more\nand more Americans kick the\nhabit each year for health\nreasons, needs to survive in the\nfuture. About balf of all\nsmokers start by age 13.\nClearly, cigarette makers have\na lot at stake.\n\nIs the industry writing off ~\nthese potential smokers?\nHardly. It\u2019s more likely that\ncigarette makers hope to pick\nthem up after they hit 18.\nAfter all, government studies\nshow, nearly 40 percent of all\nsmokers begin between age 18\nand 21.\nAnd it\u2019s pretty certain the\nindustry was paying attention\nthis year when Congress nearly\n\napproved a bill that would\nhave wiped out cigarette\nadvertising as it\u2019s now known.\nCongress also is considering\nlegislation to ban cigarette\nvending machines altogether.\nSo it makes good economic\nand political sense for the\nindustry to voluntarily do\nsomething about young\nsmokers before the\ngovernment moves in.\n\u2018The industry's campaign\ninvolves support for a national\negal smoking age of at least\n18, restrictions on minors\u2019~\n\nTI KU 000010486", "words": [{"text": "\u2018TIMN", "x0": 111, "y0": 912, "x1": 171, "y1": 932, "conf": 0.0}, {"text": "0131486", "x0": 181, "y0": 903, "x1": 260, "y1": 926, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "El", "x0": 225, "y0": 981, "x1": 773